From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hotty Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 11:26:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 15/62] blitz-paint: a box is painted on device pixels, and an
 inset ring's spread is whole

At a fractional scale a box laid out on whole CSS pixels sits at a
fraction of a device pixel: at 1.6 a box 4 CSS px in starts 6.4 device
px in. Its background, border, shadows and outline were drawn there,
anti-aliased, while an unblurred inset box-shadow's hole was snapped to
device pixels, so `inset 0 0 0 1px` drew 1.25 to 2.1 device pixels a
side by where the box sat, one side visibly thinner than the others.

Snap the frame an element is painted with to device pixels when its
transform is a translation, as browsers snap the boxes they paint: each
edge of the border box rounded on its own, the border keeping its
widths, and the content box left where layout put it, so text and
images do not move. The unblurred inset ring's spread is rounded down to
whole device pixels, at least one, as Stylo snaps a border's width, so
the ring is as wide on every side and as wide as a 1px border or outline
beside it: 1 device pixel for 1px at 1, 1.25, 1.5 and 1.6, 2 at 2 and
2.5, 3 at 3.
